.TH PCB-HOWTO 7
.SH NAME
pcb-howto \- laying out a board from its schematic, on one page
.SH THE LOOP
.PD 0
.IP 1. 3
In one call, read this page and the notes of the groups to lay out, in
the schematic source: grep -nE '^ *(group|note|end)' board.sch.
.IP 2. 3
Write the statements of one block.
A block alike is a copy: write the first, then one line for each
other, block GROUP as GROUP, and place its root (fix main.ch2.jack.j
at ...); the copy needs no other statement.
.nf
block main.ch1
block main.ch2 as main.ch1
block main.ch3 as main.ch1
.fi
.IP 3. 3
Write and run in the same call: append with a here-document
(cat >> board.pcb <<'EOF'), then make: pcbplace and pcbroute into
board.lock, then pcbcheck board.pcb < board.lock.
.IP 4. 3
Fix what the faults name, and run again.
Placement and routing are best effort: the same source and lock need
not give the same places or copper, so judge the board by its check.
.IP 5. 3
Stop when it checks clean: pcbcheck prints nothing and exits 0.
.PD
.SH EXAMPLE
An op amp, its feedback resistor between amp.in- and amp.out (so
main.filt.rf.2 is on the output) and a capacitor at its supply pin v+,
the group filt on the schematic's page main:
.PP
.nf
# filt begins
block main.filt
fix main.filt.amp at 20 18 rot 270
place main.filt.rf left of main.filt.amp
place main.filt.c down from main.filt.amp.v+
ref main.filt.c below
width main.filt.amp.out main.filt.rf.2 0.4
.fi
.PP
A clean make prints nothing: no fault, no note.
.SH STATEMENTS
.nf
fix PART at X Y [rot R] [bottom]      only where the mechanics put it
place PART WAY of PART [GAP] [rot R]  next to it, GAP mm apart (0)
place PART WAY from PIN [rot R]       its pad of the pin's net by the pin
place PART|GROUP near THING [WAY] [GAP]  the nearest clear room by a
                                      part, group, pin, cutout N or edge
block GROUP                           laid out alone, then moved whole
block GROUP as GROUP                  a copy of the second block
path NET PIN PIN...                   the net's pins wired in this order
width NET W | width PIN PIN W         track width, net or one stretch
class CLASS width W                   track width of a class of nets
via PIN WAY                           the way its plane via goes
ref PART SIDE [start|end] [rot 90]    its designator, by its courtyard
unplaced GROUP                        its parts off the board for now
.fi
.PP
A pin is PART.PIN, the part named by its page and group path:
.nf
width outputs.ch1.gain.amp.out outputs.ch1.gain.rf.2 0.635
path outputs.ch1.term.out outputs.ch1.term.r2.2 outputs.ch1.jack.j.1
.fi
NET is a net name of the netlist (grep '^net' out/board.nl).
.SH DIRECTIONS
The board is seen from the top, x to the right and y up:
.B right
is +x,
.B up
+y,
.B left
\-x,
.B down
\-y.
A ref SIDE is above, below, left or right.
rot R turns a part R degrees (90, 180 or 270) counterclockwise, seen
from the top: rot 90 turns its +x side to +y.
Without rot, a place statement turns the part, of four ways, to bring
its pads nearest their nets' pads already placed; a fixed part without
rot stands unturned.
.SH FAULTS
A fault is a line FILE:LINE: or FILE:, then what is wrong; pcbcheck
ends its line with the point, at X Y.
Parts appear by name and designator, as outputs.ch1.gain.rf (R401); a
pad as outputs.ch1.gain.rf.1 (R401).
Faults name what is in the way: both courtyards that overlap, both
pieces of copper too near and their distance, the part a fixed part is
not clear of, what blocks a plane via.
A net with no way names its two pads and the point where the search
stopped; no room for a part WAY of its anchor means it ran off the
board that way.
Move or turn the part named, or edit the statement of the line, and
run again.
.SH SEE ALSO
pcb(5), pcbplace(1), pcbroute(1), pcbcheck(1), pcbfp(5), netlist(5),
sch(5)
